  "state": {
    "case_id": "350",
    "case_summary": "A patient in their 70s with a medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension presented to the emergency department with fatigue, light-headedness, and dyspnea on exertion during the previous week.\n\nThe patient was alert and in no acute distress with a heart rate of 47 beats per minute and blood pressure of 148/59 mm Hg.\n\n- ECG findings:  Image Title: 12-Lead Electrocardiogram and the Intracardiac Recordings  Image Description: A) Normal sinus rhythm at 72 beats per minute. PR interval prolongs between first and second conducted beats. Some P waves do not conduct to ventricles. Right bundle-branch block present.  B) Surface recordings (leads I, II, and V1) with prolonged His-ventricular (HV) interval of 75 milliseconds on first conducted beat, extending to 277 milliseconds on second conducted beat.- Echocardiogram:  Demonstrated normal biventricular function without significant valvular lesions.- Electrophysiology Study:  Surface ECG showed sinus rhythm. Intracardiac recordings from bundle of His and right ventricle showed prolonged His-ventricular interval.",
